WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:01.210
Maintaining
good mental health is

00:00:01.293 --> 00:00:02.461
a mixture of boundaries

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:04.254
and doing things
that bring you joy.

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:06.089
This is embarrassing.

00:00:06.173 --> 00:00:07.382
I'm loving this!

00:00:07.466 --> 00:00:10.177
That, for me, is the most
iconic bit of the show.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.846
That's the bit… I've got
that saved on YouTube.

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:21.063
Hello, and welcome to
Sex Education Unpacked.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:24.066
I'm your esteemed host,
Gena-Mour Barrett.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:25.400
Now, let's be honest.

00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:27.152
We were left with
some pretty big questions

00:00:27.236 --> 00:00:28.737
at the end of season two.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.073
Will Maeve find out
about the deleted voicemail?

00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:33.659
Who's going to be
the new headteacher?

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:36.787
And will Otis
ever get a new jacket?

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.747
Honestly,
that is my main question.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.207
I'm not going to spoil it
for you

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.167
but, if you haven't binged
the new series yet,

00:00:42.251 --> 00:00:44.419
you might want to do it now
before someone else does.

00:00:45.087 --> 00:00:47.631
Now, one of the big themes
of this season is

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:48.715
mental health.

00:00:48.799 --> 00:00:49.675
Whether we're talking about

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:51.552
Jackson struggling
with anxiety

00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:54.137
or Aimee continuing to work
towards healing her trauma,

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:56.473
the show tackles
some tricky topics

00:00:56.557 --> 00:00:58.475
when it comes
to matters of the mind.

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:00.561
In this episode,
we're going to be discussing

00:01:00.644 --> 00:01:02.271
why being open
about these topics

00:01:02.354 --> 00:01:03.313
is so important,

00:01:03.397 --> 00:01:06.400
not just in Sex Ed,
but in our own lives, too.

00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:07.651
Joining me today are

00:01:08.235 --> 00:01:10.070
Sex Education creator,
Laurie Nunn,

00:01:10.571 --> 00:01:13.824
plus musician and actor,
Jordan Stephens,

00:01:14.825 --> 00:01:17.369
writer and advocate,
Kenny Ethan Jones,

00:01:18.412 --> 00:01:20.372
and founder of
Black Minds Matter,

00:01:20.455 --> 00:01:21.748
Agnes Mwakatuma.

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:25.627
Jordan, Kenny and Agnes,

00:01:25.711 --> 00:01:28.130
welcome to the
Sex Education Unpacked sofa.

00:01:28.213 --> 00:01:29.923
-Hello!
-Hello.

00:01:30.007 --> 00:01:32.176
In season two,
we saw Jackson struggle

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:34.011
under the pressure
of his sporting career,

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:35.721
leading him to
experience anxiety,

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:37.598
which is something
he opens up to Cal about

00:01:37.681 --> 00:01:38.807
in season three.

00:01:38.891 --> 00:01:39.850
Let's watch a clip.

00:01:40.726 --> 00:01:42.227
So, why did you go
all woozy today?

00:01:45.022 --> 00:01:45.898
It's anxiety.

00:01:46.565 --> 00:01:47.816
I've always had it.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.193
But it's been
pretty bad recently.

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:51.320
I think,
now I'm not swimming,

00:01:51.403 --> 00:01:52.487
I haven't got a routine.

00:01:54.156 --> 00:01:56.033
And I get triggered
by the most random stuff.

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:58.368
It's ok. I have anxiety too,
so I get it.

00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:00.704
But what was the trigger
this time?

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:01.872
Just school.

00:02:02.372 --> 00:02:04.082
I was thinking,
if I don't swim anymore

00:02:04.166 --> 00:02:05.250
and I'm not head boy,

00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:07.169
and you might have heard
I'm not very good at acting,

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:09.213
then just who the hell am I?

00:02:10.464 --> 00:02:12.591
Like, maybe I don't care
about anything enough.

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:13.592
Woah. You're 17.

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:14.968
You don't have to have it
all figured out.

00:02:16.345 --> 00:02:18.055
This is not the message
the world is giving me.

00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:20.432
Maybe try just being
Jackson Marchetti

00:02:20.516 --> 00:02:21.391
for a little bit.

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.395
Alright, I'll try.

00:02:26.897 --> 00:02:28.732
There's an interesting line
there from Cal:

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:31.735
"You're 17. You don't have
to have it all figured out."

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.070
Do you think that,
sometimes,

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.821
our mental health
can be impacted

00:02:34.905 --> 00:02:37.741
by the pressure
to have everything sorted?

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:39.117
Oh my God, yeah.

00:02:39.201 --> 00:02:40.202
I feel like
it's getting worse.

00:02:40.285 --> 00:02:43.789
There's more of
an expectation to be

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:45.040
earning a lot of money,

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:47.793
to be the most beautiful,
to be…

00:02:47.876 --> 00:02:49.336
to have a total purpose,

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:52.339
and it's, yeah,
poisoned, I suppose,

00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:56.009
our ability to
feel ok with just

00:02:56.635 --> 00:02:59.304
allowing,
giving ourselves some time.

00:02:59.388 --> 00:03:01.014
I honestly think

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:03.600
the feeling of inadequacy

00:03:03.684 --> 00:03:05.811
might be at
an all-time high.

00:03:06.353 --> 00:03:08.647
Because we're constantly
in touch with

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.900
examples of lives that
we compare ourselves to.

00:03:11.984 --> 00:03:13.944
-Social media.
-Yeah. Social media and--

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:15.279
I think you get that a lot.

00:03:15.362 --> 00:03:17.781
A lot of our clients are
finding it incredibly hard,

00:03:17.865 --> 00:03:20.617
especially during
a time like the pandemic,

00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:23.078
like seeing people achieve
so much

00:03:23.161 --> 00:03:25.497
and then feeling like
you haven't done anything

00:03:25.581 --> 00:03:26.707
in a year.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.252
That pressure is a lot
for a lot of people.

00:03:30.878 --> 00:03:32.963
There are also these,

00:03:33.839 --> 00:03:36.049
kind of,
accepted limitations

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:37.593
that aren't even
necessarily true,

00:03:37.676 --> 00:03:39.011
so, again, for example,

00:03:39.094 --> 00:03:41.305
the age you're expected
to have certain things.

00:03:41.388 --> 00:03:42.723
Maybe be able to
get a house,

00:03:42.806 --> 00:03:44.391
have a relationship, kids.

00:03:44.474 --> 00:03:46.143
Yeah, there are all these

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:47.769
ideas of
where you should be,

00:03:47.853 --> 00:03:49.146
when, in reality, you know,

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:50.689
everybody's life path is
different.

00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:53.108
What they're able to
achieve,

00:03:53.192 --> 00:03:54.943
depending on their
circumstances, is different,

00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:57.738
and the dream is for people
to be ok with that.

00:03:57.821 --> 00:03:59.907
Kenny, for you,
how has your relationship

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.034
with your mental health
changed over time?

00:04:02.576 --> 00:04:05.287
It's been through
so many different stages.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.415
When I was younger, one of
the things I commonly said

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:09.458
was I just want to be happy.

00:04:09.541 --> 00:04:11.418
And I didn't know
what that meant

00:04:11.502 --> 00:04:12.377
because, like you said,

00:04:12.461 --> 00:04:13.420
there were
so many expectations

00:04:13.504 --> 00:04:15.380
of what happiness
and success kind of meant.

00:04:16.465 --> 00:04:18.008
But, as I got older,
it was more about

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:20.594
being at peace with myself
and my decisions.

00:04:21.303 --> 00:04:23.388
And just being, like,
content in who I am

00:04:23.472 --> 00:04:24.806
and my identity and,
kind of,

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.725
how the world views me
as a trans man.

00:04:26.808 --> 00:04:28.644
When you're subjected to
transphobia and racism,

00:04:28.727 --> 00:04:29.978
it's hard to navigate
as well as

00:04:30.062 --> 00:04:31.230
just being a teenager

00:04:31.313 --> 00:04:33.273
and navigating
those normal things.

00:04:34.358 --> 00:04:36.193
So I've got really strict
with myself

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:37.528
in terms of boundaries
that I set

00:04:37.611 --> 00:04:38.779
to protect my mental health.

00:04:38.862 --> 00:04:40.113
That's probably been
the biggest change

00:04:40.197 --> 00:04:41.114
over the years.

00:04:41.198 --> 00:04:42.908
-Boundaries are essential.
-So essential.

00:04:42.991 --> 00:04:44.576
I feel like maintaining
good mental health

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.827
is a mixture of boundaries

00:04:45.911 --> 00:04:48.080
and doing things
that bring you joy.

00:04:48.163 --> 00:04:49.957
For me, it's like stepping
away from conversations

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:50.958
that I don't want to be in,

00:04:51.041 --> 00:04:52.167
not giving energy to people

00:04:52.251 --> 00:04:53.794
that I don't want to
give it to,

00:04:53.877 --> 00:04:56.630
taking time to just go
for a drive or a bike ride,

00:04:57.339 --> 00:04:58.882
and just doing little things
that bring me joy,

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:00.133
like playing with my dog.

00:05:00.217 --> 00:05:01.426
It looks different
for everybody.

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:02.469
Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:02.553 --> 00:05:03.971
-Self-care.
-Yeah.

00:05:04.054 --> 00:05:07.015
The word "boundaries" seems
to resonate with all of you.

00:05:07.099 --> 00:05:09.393
What's been the biggest
learning for any of you?

00:05:09.476 --> 00:05:10.477
Maybe I can start with you,
Agnes.

00:05:10.561 --> 00:05:11.895
In terms
of your mental health

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:13.146
and your own
mental health journey.

00:05:14.189 --> 00:05:16.400
One of the biggest learnings
has been

00:05:16.483 --> 00:05:17.860
that is really takes
a village.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.945
That I can't do it
on my own.

00:05:20.821 --> 00:05:24.032
I think surrendering
to accepting the fact that,

00:05:24.825 --> 00:05:27.327
if I need to get through
some tough times,

00:05:27.411 --> 00:05:28.954
I'm going to need
some people around me

00:05:29.037 --> 00:05:30.080
to help me get through that.

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:32.416
Leaning how to trust.
Learning how to love.

00:05:32.499 --> 00:05:33.750
Learning how to listen.

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:36.003
And allowing other people
in.

00:05:36.086 --> 00:05:37.379
-Love that.
-I love that, too.

00:05:38.213 --> 00:05:40.799
Sometimes it helps
to write down

00:05:40.883 --> 00:05:42.467
what you want to say first.

00:05:42.551 --> 00:05:43.844
I'm not good at writing.

00:05:43.927 --> 00:05:46.638
Or you can ask the other
person to close their eyes

00:05:46.722 --> 00:05:47.973
whilst you talk to them.

00:05:48.056 --> 00:05:49.224
Sounds stupid.

00:05:49.308 --> 00:05:51.685
What I'm saying is
it might help you

00:05:51.768 --> 00:05:54.188
think more clearly about
what you want to say

00:05:54.271 --> 00:05:55.522
if you don't feel observed.

00:05:57.065 --> 00:05:58.233
It's just a suggestion.

00:05:59.568 --> 00:06:00.819
I'm going now.

00:06:02.321 --> 00:06:03.864
You should wear
your normal clothes again.

00:06:07.034 --> 00:06:09.286
That top makes your neck
look very long.

00:06:09.786 --> 00:06:13.207
I think boundaries have been

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.542
my most recent
accomplishment

00:06:15.626 --> 00:06:19.254
in terms of self-care
and self-preservation.

00:06:20.464 --> 00:06:22.966
I often say yes
quite quickly to things

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:24.051
because I'm excitable.

00:06:24.134 --> 00:06:25.594
I'm like a little chocolate
labrador, you know?

00:06:27.763 --> 00:06:31.475
And I love
caring for others,

00:06:31.558 --> 00:06:33.185
which is not a bad thing,

00:06:33.268 --> 00:06:35.437
but it's important
to understand

00:06:35.521 --> 00:06:38.273
that you do actually need to
take care of yourself first.

00:06:38.357 --> 00:06:40.484
Not to an extreme. All of
this is in moderation.

00:06:41.068 --> 00:06:42.486
You sort of touched on it
a little bit here

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:43.445
for my next question,

00:06:43.529 --> 00:06:46.073
which is, if you feel
comfortable to share,

00:06:46.156 --> 00:06:48.367
what are some of your own
coping strategies

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.285
if you're having
a bad mental health day?

00:06:51.161 --> 00:06:53.330
I have an emergency
state of health list

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:54.540
that I have on my phone,

00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:55.916
so any time it gets
to a point

00:06:55.999 --> 00:06:58.418
where I'm really unable to
take care of myself…

00:06:58.502 --> 00:07:00.045
Things like reach out
to one person

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:01.171
and tell them how I feel,

00:07:02.548 --> 00:07:04.675
buy microwave noodles,
and things like that,

00:07:04.758 --> 00:07:05.884
because I don't tend to
cook foods

00:07:05.968 --> 00:07:07.386
because I don't want to
take care of myself,

00:07:07.469 --> 00:07:08.470
but I need to eat…

00:07:08.554 --> 00:07:10.264
-So you can quickly--
-Yeah. Quickly make.

00:07:10.347 --> 00:07:13.308
So just being smart with
how I know my body reacts.

00:07:13.392 --> 00:07:15.227
Even small things like
making sure that I shower

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:16.186
or brush my teeth

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:17.980
because, when you're
in a bad, bad place,

00:07:18.063 --> 00:07:19.481
that's the first thing
to go.

00:07:19.565 --> 00:07:20.649
Do you know what I mean?

00:07:20.732 --> 00:07:22.526
Other things like making
sure I go for walks.

00:07:22.609 --> 00:07:24.820
I try to plan as much
self-care in that day

00:07:24.903 --> 00:07:26.530
as possible, so that…

00:07:26.613 --> 00:07:28.782
I feel like if you fill your
days with more good things,

00:07:29.366 --> 00:07:32.244
slowly, but surely,
you'll start to feel better.

00:07:32.327 --> 00:07:34.162
I feel like if you don't
invest into good habits,

00:07:34.246 --> 00:07:36.748
all you're doing is kind of
sitting in the same place

00:07:36.832 --> 00:07:37.749
and I don't think
that helps.

00:07:37.833 --> 00:07:39.376
I feel like that just
helps you spiral.

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:42.171
I think, for me,
it's knowing my triggers.

00:07:42.254 --> 00:07:43.088
I know that,

00:07:43.172 --> 00:07:45.507
if I'm going through really
bad mental health,

00:07:45.591 --> 00:07:47.676
I know the first thing
that I pick up is

00:07:47.759 --> 00:07:49.803
either going to make me
spiral

00:07:49.887 --> 00:07:51.305
or it's going to help me.

00:07:51.388 --> 00:07:52.723
So I just ensure that

00:07:52.806 --> 00:07:54.766
the first thing
that I start doing

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:55.976
is actually good
for my body.

00:07:56.059 --> 00:07:57.895
Whether that means starting
with a healthy meal

00:07:57.978 --> 00:08:00.397
that means
I'm very unlikely going to

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:01.607
spiral into something else,

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:04.443
whereas if I order
McDonalds and Deliveroo,

00:08:04.526 --> 00:08:05.736
that means
McDonalds and Deliveroo

00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:07.154
until the day
that I feel better.

00:08:07.237 --> 00:08:08.822
It just becomes too much.

00:08:08.906 --> 00:08:11.658
Or if it's one glass of
wine, then it's two bottles.

00:08:11.742 --> 00:08:12.784
You know?

00:08:12.868 --> 00:08:14.203
There's no in-between.

00:08:14.286 --> 00:08:15.245
So I just have to
make sure that

00:08:15.329 --> 00:08:16.622
the first thing that I do

00:08:16.705 --> 00:08:19.499
is good for me,
otherwise that's it.

00:08:19.583 --> 00:08:20.626
It's a downward spiral.

00:08:20.709 --> 00:08:22.211
You sort of touched on this
a little bit earlier

00:08:22.294 --> 00:08:24.129
when you mentioned
social media,

00:08:24.213 --> 00:08:26.131
because people
obviously talk a lot about

00:08:26.215 --> 00:08:27.299
mental health pressures

00:08:27.382 --> 00:08:29.343
that come from
living our lives online.

00:08:29.885 --> 00:08:32.721
Do any of you find social
media can have an impact

00:08:32.804 --> 00:08:34.139
on your personal
mental health?

00:08:34.723 --> 00:08:36.225
-Yeah, definitely.
-Yes.

00:08:36.725 --> 00:08:38.894
Kenny, you offered
an emphatic yes.

00:08:38.977 --> 00:08:39.895
Could you elaborate on that?

00:08:39.978 --> 00:08:42.105
Honestly, like, there's
just no space for nuance.

00:08:43.106 --> 00:08:45.609
You can't talk about
the grey on social media.

00:08:45.692 --> 00:08:47.653
You can't explain that
in a caption.

00:08:47.736 --> 00:08:49.613
And so,
you either find yourself

00:08:49.696 --> 00:08:51.990
swinging left or swinging
right in situations,

00:08:52.074 --> 00:08:54.743
and I feel like it's easy
to lose yourself in that.

00:08:54.826 --> 00:08:57.287
Because I'm a person
who really respects the grey

00:08:57.371 --> 00:08:59.039
and wants
the difficult conversation

00:08:59.122 --> 00:09:00.499
and to actually get to
the bottom of things

00:09:00.582 --> 00:09:01.959
and not just agree
for the sake of agreeing.

00:09:02.584 --> 00:09:04.461
And you can't do that
on social media.

00:09:04.545 --> 00:09:05.879
Again, it goes back
to boundaries

00:09:05.963 --> 00:09:07.464
and thinking, you know,
making sure that

00:09:07.548 --> 00:09:09.174
I stand strong
in my personal opinion

00:09:09.258 --> 00:09:11.426
and not being swayed
by the conversation

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:12.886
or because
it's more popular.

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:15.097
-Do you know what I mean?
-Or the urgency.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:16.807
-Yeah.
-Everyone to react to stuff.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:17.766
To react. Absolutely.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.143
I haven't even had a chance
to even catch up

00:09:20.227 --> 00:09:21.103
on what's going on.

00:09:21.186 --> 00:09:22.437
And, also, you shouldn't
speak about things

00:09:22.521 --> 00:09:23.647
that you don't know about.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.691
Agnes,
can you tell us a bit about

00:09:25.774 --> 00:09:27.317
setting up
Black Minds Matter

00:09:27.401 --> 00:09:28.652
and the specific issues that

00:09:28.735 --> 00:09:30.195
young, black people
might have

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:31.697
when it comes to
speaking out?

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.532
There is this stigma
that exists

00:09:33.615 --> 00:09:34.658
in the black community.

00:09:35.158 --> 00:09:36.535
I don't like
talking about it too much

00:09:36.618 --> 00:09:37.703
because,
at the end of the day,

00:09:37.786 --> 00:09:39.288
we have a right
to have the stigma.

00:09:39.371 --> 00:09:40.455
We've been failed
so many times

00:09:40.539 --> 00:09:42.082
by the healthcare system

00:09:42.165 --> 00:09:44.084
that you can't blame people
for having a reluctance

00:09:44.168 --> 00:09:45.043
to speak on stuff.

00:09:45.127 --> 00:09:46.336
However,

00:09:47.504 --> 00:09:48.755
in a lot of black,

00:09:48.839 --> 00:09:50.299
especially African,
families,

00:09:50.382 --> 00:09:53.385
children are not awarded
the space to be listened to

00:09:53.468 --> 00:09:54.761
as much as possible.

00:09:55.262 --> 00:09:56.972
And we also have
this tendency,

00:09:57.055 --> 00:09:58.307
especially
if you're religious,

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:00.225
to take your problems
to God.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:02.686
And no one else.

00:10:02.769 --> 00:10:05.063
So it can be really
difficult for children,

00:10:05.147 --> 00:10:07.191
especially children
who are experiencing

00:10:07.274 --> 00:10:08.400
modern-day issues,

00:10:08.483 --> 00:10:10.319
issues that
African parents are

00:10:10.402 --> 00:10:12.237
just not used to
talking about.

00:10:13.822 --> 00:10:16.867
It can be incredibly
difficult for them

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.661
to even be willing to open
up to their own parents.

00:10:19.745 --> 00:10:21.330
And, sometimes,
we have clients

00:10:21.413 --> 00:10:23.707
who, for example,
apply for therapy

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:24.917
and they don't have
a safe space

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:26.418
to discuss their problems.

00:10:26.502 --> 00:10:28.337
And that is just so sad

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:29.630
because the service is
there,

00:10:29.713 --> 00:10:32.132
but these kids don't feel
comfortable talking

00:10:32.216 --> 00:10:33.759
because mum or dad is
in the other room.

00:10:34.343 --> 00:10:37.471
And that's something that
I think, as a community,

00:10:37.554 --> 00:10:38.597
we really need to talk
more about.

00:10:39.097 --> 00:10:40.641
So, why did you go
all woozy today?

00:10:43.268 --> 00:10:44.311
It's anxiety.

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:46.104
I've always had it.

00:10:46.188 --> 00:10:47.606
But it's been
pretty bad recently.

00:10:48.649 --> 00:10:49.775
I think,
now I'm not swimming,

00:10:49.858 --> 00:10:50.859
I haven't got a routine.

00:10:52.569 --> 00:10:54.446
And I get triggered
by the most random stuff.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.698
It's ok. I have anxiety too,
so I get it.

00:10:57.282 --> 00:10:59.493
So, we know things
are changing slowly,

00:10:59.576 --> 00:11:01.245
but how would you all
like to see

00:11:01.328 --> 00:11:04.498
the conversation around
mental health going forward?

00:11:04.581 --> 00:11:06.667
What more needs to be done?

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.418
Agnes, I'd love to go to
you first for this one.

00:11:08.502 --> 00:11:09.878
I think we need to get
more comfortable with

00:11:09.962 --> 00:11:11.588
talking about
serious mental health.

00:11:11.672 --> 00:11:13.757
Everyone's talking about
anxiety and depression,

00:11:13.841 --> 00:11:16.468
but as soon as conversations
about schizophrenia,

00:11:16.552 --> 00:11:17.970
psychosis, PTSD,

00:11:18.053 --> 00:11:19.555
severe PTSD, I mean…

00:11:19.638 --> 00:11:21.682
Aimee, bless her,
couldn't even get on a bus

00:11:21.765 --> 00:11:23.267
after what had happened.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.811
Everyone kind of
shies away from that.

00:11:25.894 --> 00:11:27.604
And, when you look at
black people, for example,

00:11:27.688 --> 00:11:28.939
we're seven times
more likely to

00:11:29.022 --> 00:11:30.566
be diagnosed with psychosis.

00:11:30.649 --> 00:11:32.526
It's time that
we start talking about

00:11:32.609 --> 00:11:34.736
you know, those triggers
and what that means,

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.780
and stop completely, like,

00:11:36.864 --> 00:11:38.615
demonising it
in the black community

00:11:38.699 --> 00:11:40.450
because we've had it
time and time again,

00:11:40.534 --> 00:11:43.996
how people kind of assume,
for example, that

00:11:44.788 --> 00:11:46.540
someone is possessed,
for example.

00:11:46.623 --> 00:11:48.250
Just kind of
changing the languages

00:11:48.333 --> 00:11:50.419
and the ways that we talk
and describe people

00:11:50.502 --> 00:11:52.504
that suffer from
severe mental health.

00:11:52.588 --> 00:11:54.673
I think that that is
definitely the one big thing

00:11:54.756 --> 00:11:56.508
that I would like to
see change.

00:11:57.509 --> 00:11:58.927
I think
it's organisations for me.

00:11:59.011 --> 00:12:00.596
Like Black Minds Matter.

00:12:00.679 --> 00:12:02.139
I would love to see
more charities

00:12:02.222 --> 00:12:03.182
and organisations like that

00:12:03.265 --> 00:12:05.475
because I feel like, you
know, as a black trans man,

00:12:05.559 --> 00:12:07.477
I want to talk to someone
who's either black or trans

00:12:07.561 --> 00:12:09.438
because they're going to
have more understanding of

00:12:09.521 --> 00:12:10.647
my lived experience,

00:12:10.731 --> 00:12:12.316
so connecting there

00:12:12.399 --> 00:12:14.193
and making sense of
my lived experience

00:12:14.276 --> 00:12:16.570
and just, like, guiding me
on a good journey

00:12:16.653 --> 00:12:18.572
and making me more positive
and all those things.

00:12:18.655 --> 00:12:20.741
I just think
it's reliant on, like…

00:12:20.824 --> 00:12:21.700
Because, I'll be honest,

00:12:21.783 --> 00:12:22.951
if I'm talking to
a white person,

00:12:23.035 --> 00:12:25.370
sometimes we just don't have
the same experience.

00:12:25.454 --> 00:12:26.663
And so they don't understand

00:12:26.747 --> 00:12:28.498
and I spend so much of
my time educating them

00:12:28.582 --> 00:12:30.250
to get them to the point
where they even understand,

00:12:30.334 --> 00:12:32.377
and it's like I don't want
to spend my days educating.

00:12:32.461 --> 00:12:34.046
I just want support
and help.

00:12:34.129 --> 00:12:36.507
And so I think it's,
yeah, just more specific

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.300
charities and organisations

00:12:38.383 --> 00:12:39.968
to help
more marginalised groups.

00:12:41.136 --> 00:12:42.137
And the intersections of

00:12:42.221 --> 00:12:44.014
all of those
marginalised groups also.

00:12:44.097 --> 00:12:45.432
So, yeah.
I'd really love to see that.

00:12:45.516 --> 00:12:46.767
It's unacceptable
at this point

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:47.768
that there isn't, like,

00:12:47.851 --> 00:12:50.145
a holistic approach
to schooling, either.

00:12:50.229 --> 00:12:51.813
I think this can start
a lot earlier.

00:12:51.897 --> 00:12:55.484
There is a massive, massive,
massive elephant in the room

00:12:55.567 --> 00:12:56.818
there too in school,

00:12:56.902 --> 00:12:59.613
where there is absolutely no
reason why you shouldn't be

00:12:59.696 --> 00:13:01.782
educated about your own body
and the people around you

00:13:01.865 --> 00:13:02.866
when you're developing.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:03.951
It's insane to me.

00:13:04.034 --> 00:13:05.661
There are reports…

00:13:05.744 --> 00:13:07.621
I remember reading something
about a school in Harlem

00:13:07.704 --> 00:13:09.915
that replaced detention
with meditation

00:13:09.998 --> 00:13:13.752
and the
behavioural discrepancies

00:13:13.836 --> 00:13:15.963
changed dramatically in that

00:13:16.046 --> 00:13:18.215
because you're not
punishing someone,

00:13:18.298 --> 00:13:19.675
you're not forcing them
into a space where…

00:13:19.758 --> 00:13:20.717
You're actually
allowing them to

00:13:20.801 --> 00:13:22.177
communicate
with their own bodies.

00:13:22.261 --> 00:13:24.680
It is beyond comprehension
that

00:13:24.763 --> 00:13:26.223
that isn't already a thing.

00:13:26.306 --> 00:13:28.308
This was fantastic,
honestly. Thank you so…

00:13:28.392 --> 00:13:30.477
Honestly,
Jordan, Kenny, Agnes,

00:13:30.561 --> 00:13:31.436
thank you so much.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:32.396
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:13:32.479 --> 00:13:33.522
That was great.

00:13:36.024 --> 00:13:37.693
Agnes, thanks for staying

00:13:37.776 --> 00:13:39.611
on the
Sex Education Unpacked sofa.

00:13:39.695 --> 00:13:42.114
We've also been joined
by the woman behind it all,

00:13:42.197 --> 00:13:43.073
Laurie Nunn.

00:13:43.156 --> 00:13:45.075
-Laurie, hello!
-Hi.

00:13:45.158 --> 00:13:46.034
In season three,

00:13:46.118 --> 00:13:48.495
we see Aimee continue
to heal from the trauma

00:13:48.579 --> 00:13:50.205
of being sexually assaulted
on the bus.

00:13:50.289 --> 00:13:52.749
She goes to therapy, learns
to love her body again

00:13:52.833 --> 00:13:54.585
and begins to engage
with feminism.

00:13:54.668 --> 00:13:56.044
Let's see her in action.

00:13:57.504 --> 00:13:58.630
Sorry.

00:13:58.714 --> 00:14:01.300
I just find it hard to talk
about it sometimes.

00:14:01.383 --> 00:14:02.301
That's ok.

00:14:02.885 --> 00:14:05.345
We don't have to talk about
anything you don't want to.

00:14:05.429 --> 00:14:07.264
I just want to
be the old me again.

00:14:07.347 --> 00:14:10.225
Well, you may never be
the old you, Aimee.

00:14:11.185 --> 00:14:12.060
But that's ok.

00:14:12.644 --> 00:14:13.770
As human beings,

00:14:13.854 --> 00:14:16.023
we are constantly changing
and developing.

00:14:16.732 --> 00:14:19.568
And,
by processing this trauma,

00:14:19.651 --> 00:14:22.321
you may gain clarity
on the event itself,

00:14:22.404 --> 00:14:24.364
and we can move you towards

00:14:24.448 --> 00:14:27.117
healing the relationship
with your body again.

00:14:27.868 --> 00:14:28.785
Would you like that?

00:14:29.369 --> 00:14:30.370
Yeah, I'd like that.

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:34.291
Laurie, when thinking about
this storyline,

00:14:34.374 --> 00:14:37.169
were you inspired by wider
movements such as Me Too?

00:14:37.753 --> 00:14:41.757
The story for Aimee
series two strand

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:43.634
came from
a really personal experience

00:14:43.717 --> 00:14:44.968
in my own life.

00:14:45.052 --> 00:14:47.721
So, I was sexually assaulted
on a local bus

00:14:48.514 --> 00:14:51.141
and I knew that that was
something that I wanted to

00:14:51.225 --> 00:14:53.644
kind of explore
through writing the show

00:14:53.727 --> 00:14:55.938
and I knew that Aimee was
an interesting character

00:14:56.021 --> 00:14:56.897
to do that with.

00:14:58.815 --> 00:15:00.734
And then,
moving into series three,

00:15:00.817 --> 00:15:04.404
it felt really important
to me that her strand

00:15:04.488 --> 00:15:05.948
just didn't fall away,

00:15:06.031 --> 00:15:07.282
and that it…

00:15:07.366 --> 00:15:08.825
I wanted to show that,
you know,

00:15:08.909 --> 00:15:10.911
if you've experienced
trauma,

00:15:10.994 --> 00:15:12.454
but, particularly,
sexual trauma,

00:15:12.538 --> 00:15:13.997
it's not something
that just disappears.

00:15:14.081 --> 00:15:14.957
It's something that you…

00:15:15.707 --> 00:15:17.709
you know,
continue to carry with you

00:15:17.793 --> 00:15:18.669
throughout your life.

00:15:19.169 --> 00:15:22.381
And, yeah, in terms of
the Me Too movement,

00:15:22.464 --> 00:15:25.509
I don't think that it
necessarily, like,

00:15:25.592 --> 00:15:26.802
inspired me to write it,

00:15:27.386 --> 00:15:30.013
but I think that
that movement has given

00:15:30.097 --> 00:15:31.974
myself and so many women,

00:15:32.057 --> 00:15:34.852
like, the confidence and
the tools and the language

00:15:34.935 --> 00:15:37.354
to reflect on
our experiences and say:

00:15:37.437 --> 00:15:39.857
"I think this is actually
a really important story

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:40.858
and I want to tell it

00:15:40.941 --> 00:15:42.734
and I think it, like,
deserves to be

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:44.736
seen and spoken about."

00:15:45.529 --> 00:15:48.282
One of the things
that I love so much

00:15:48.365 --> 00:15:50.784
about the way
that Aimee's storyline

00:15:50.868 --> 00:15:52.369
happens in season three

00:15:52.452 --> 00:15:53.745
is that it doesn't just
fall away.

00:15:53.829 --> 00:15:55.122
I think that,
in other shows,

00:15:55.205 --> 00:15:57.165
perhaps it would still be
present within flashbacks

00:15:57.249 --> 00:15:58.250
or things like that,

00:15:58.333 --> 00:16:00.836
but you actually see
Aimee go to therapy

00:16:00.919 --> 00:16:03.589
and that is a really
important change

00:16:03.672 --> 00:16:05.007
and moment
for that character.

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.718
Agnes, it's interesting
what Aimee says

00:16:07.801 --> 00:16:09.386
about wanting
to be the old her.

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:12.472
Why can sexual trauma be
so difficult

00:16:12.556 --> 00:16:14.099
for women to move forward
from?

00:16:14.183 --> 00:16:17.603
One of the annoying things
about sexual assault

00:16:17.686 --> 00:16:19.438
is it starts so early.

00:16:20.981 --> 00:16:22.941
But we're not educated
enough

00:16:23.025 --> 00:16:25.319
about the different forms
of sexual assault.

00:16:25.903 --> 00:16:28.947
Like, I remember going to,
sort of like,

00:16:29.031 --> 00:16:30.574
an under 16s nightclub

00:16:30.657 --> 00:16:34.661
and having the bouncer
grope me as I was walking in

00:16:34.745 --> 00:16:36.371
and I didn't think
that was a bad thing.

00:16:36.455 --> 00:16:39.208
But I'm under 16.
He should not be doing that.

00:16:39.291 --> 00:16:42.085
And it took me a very
long time to accept that.

00:16:42.753 --> 00:16:45.464
I was in this, kind of like,
long period of denial

00:16:45.547 --> 00:16:47.299
that, you know,
it wasn't that bad.

00:16:47.382 --> 00:16:49.301
I was wearing a short skirt.

00:16:49.384 --> 00:16:52.346
You know, like, he deserved
access to my body.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:55.724
I think it's just difficult
because, a lot of the time,

00:16:55.807 --> 00:16:57.100
we're in denial
for such a long time

00:16:57.184 --> 00:16:59.269
and then having to
just accept that,

00:16:59.353 --> 00:17:02.105
you know, somebody has
violated you is difficult.

00:17:02.189 --> 00:17:03.524
And then having
to accept that

00:17:03.607 --> 00:17:05.734
it's going to be a part
of you for a very long time

00:17:05.817 --> 00:17:06.860
is even harder.

00:17:07.361 --> 00:17:08.862
I think you're so right,
as well.

00:17:08.946 --> 00:17:10.656
Like, sometimes
it feels like things happen

00:17:10.739 --> 00:17:12.449
and then, by the time you've
realised it was a thing,

00:17:12.533 --> 00:17:13.408
you're like:

00:17:13.492 --> 00:17:15.244
"Oh. Is it too late for me
to even address it?"

00:17:15.327 --> 00:17:16.995
Like:
"Should I just continue?"

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:19.039
You're absolutely right
about that.

00:17:19.790 --> 00:17:23.460
Do you feel that,
if you hadn't smiled at him,

00:17:24.086 --> 00:17:25.754
he wouldn't have assaulted
you?

00:17:26.713 --> 00:17:27.589
I don't know.

00:17:28.757 --> 00:17:30.425
Had you smiled at
strangers before?

00:17:33.595 --> 00:17:35.597
And had that led to
them assaulting you?

00:17:39.226 --> 00:17:40.602
Listen to me, Aimee.

00:17:41.728 --> 00:17:44.022
What that man did to you
on the bus

00:17:44.606 --> 00:17:47.943
has nothing to do
with your smile

00:17:48.569 --> 00:17:50.237
or your personality

00:17:51.196 --> 00:17:53.657
and is only about him.

00:17:54.908 --> 00:17:59.496
And it is absolutely
not your fault.

00:18:01.373 --> 00:18:03.125
What advice
would you give to someone

00:18:03.208 --> 00:18:05.294
who is at the beginning
of that journey

00:18:05.377 --> 00:18:06.712
of understanding

00:18:07.462 --> 00:18:08.797
an experience
that's happened to them,

00:18:08.881 --> 00:18:10.299
such as Aimee's storyline?

00:18:11.383 --> 00:18:13.093
I think
the most important thing is

00:18:13.177 --> 00:18:14.845
finding people
that you trust

00:18:15.429 --> 00:18:17.598
and people
who are professionals,

00:18:17.681 --> 00:18:20.851
who understand
the various forms of trauma,

00:18:20.934 --> 00:18:22.728
especially sexual assault.

00:18:22.811 --> 00:18:24.730
Because the
most dangerous thing that

00:18:24.813 --> 00:18:26.565
you can ever do to yourself

00:18:26.648 --> 00:18:29.735
during, like, you know,
a journey of healing is

00:18:29.818 --> 00:18:32.070
share your story
with somebody that is

00:18:32.154 --> 00:18:34.198
not quite versed
on the topic

00:18:34.281 --> 00:18:37.034
and, also, that could
possibly gaslight you.

00:18:37.117 --> 00:18:38.035
Laurie,

00:18:38.118 --> 00:18:40.120
was it important for you
to have

00:18:40.204 --> 00:18:42.372
Aimee go to therapy
and actually seek out help

00:18:42.456 --> 00:18:43.498
and have that conversation?

00:18:43.582 --> 00:18:44.499
Yeah.

00:18:44.583 --> 00:18:48.462
I feel like in,
you know, in series two,

00:18:49.505 --> 00:18:52.341
she really gets the sort of
catharsis and the help

00:18:52.424 --> 00:18:55.010
from her peers, from
her female friends in, like,

00:18:55.093 --> 00:18:57.054
realising that she's not
alone in that experience.

00:18:57.137 --> 00:18:59.431
That, like, other women
in her life have had

00:18:59.515 --> 00:19:01.558
similar things happen.

00:19:01.642 --> 00:19:03.101
To me, it felt like…

00:19:04.019 --> 00:19:05.896
it felt, like,
really important to say

00:19:05.979 --> 00:19:07.439
to anyone
that's watching the show

00:19:07.523 --> 00:19:09.149
that has been through
something like that

00:19:09.233 --> 00:19:11.568
that there is a point
at which,

00:19:11.652 --> 00:19:13.695
if you're still carrying
those, like,

00:19:13.779 --> 00:19:17.574
negative feelings towards
yourself and your own body,

00:19:18.408 --> 00:19:20.452
that you should seek
professional help.

00:19:21.411 --> 00:19:23.205
And that there is,
like, a lot of

00:19:23.288 --> 00:19:25.123
services out there

00:19:25.666 --> 00:19:26.834
that can give you, you know,

00:19:26.917 --> 00:19:28.627
the tools to, like,
move forward.

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:29.586
Yeah, absolutely.

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:32.422
Agnes, Laurie. Thank you
so much for joining me.

00:19:32.506 --> 00:19:34.049
Now, I need a break.

00:19:34.132 --> 00:19:35.884
I'm sweating. I'm tired.

00:19:35.968 --> 00:19:36.885
My throat's dry.

00:19:36.969 --> 00:19:38.637
My lips are not dry because
they've got gloss on them.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:40.138
It's hard work doing
this presenting job,

00:19:40.222 --> 00:19:42.224
so I'm handing
the presenting reigns

00:19:42.307 --> 00:19:43.851
over to Jordan
for the next segment,

00:19:43.934 --> 00:19:45.269
where he's going to
quiz Laurie

00:19:45.894 --> 00:19:47.062
on the making of
Sex Education.

00:19:47.145 --> 00:19:49.523
I'll see you back in
the games room afterwards.

00:19:52.025 --> 00:19:53.277
Thanks, Gena.

00:19:53.777 --> 00:19:56.071
-Laurie, how are you?
